{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-115854",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/f26e5b61c573664f0397667aff82cfe5d918e0d22f005658862c28c53d5de748.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=2$ and $x=4.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-4)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(x-2)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$",
